<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lumumba (film, Raoul Peck)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2000</span><span class="sg-chain-h">performed in one of its scenes</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lumumba, la mort d'un prophète (documentary, Raoul Peck)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in the film</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Le Jour d'Après (Baloji, performed by Siku Ya Baadaye, on Kinshasa Succursale)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2011</span><span class="sg-chain-h">reimagined version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">La Liberté Cha Cha (Gérard Addat)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Rumba Liberté (Tabou Combo)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2011</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new version</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Music of the African diaspora, particularly Cuban music</span><span class="sg-chain-h">instruments and rhythms</span></li></ul></section>
